# PayShap Real-Time Fraud Detection — ML Pipeline

Reproducible, tracked ML pipeline for classifying PayShap instant credit
transfers (ISO 20022 `pacs.008` messaging) as fraudulent or benign, under a
real-time serving budget of **<50ms end-to-end**.

## Repository layout

```
.
├── app/                        # FastAPI real-time scoring service
│   ├── main.py                 # lifespan model loading, /predict, /health
│   └── schemas.py               # PayShapTransactionRequest/Response contracts
├── src/payshap_ml/
│   ├── pipelines/
│   │   ├── data_ingestion.py    # Stage 1: raw -> cleaned
│   │   ├── feature_engineering.py  # Stage 2: PayShap/ISO 20022 features
│   │   ├── train.py             # Stage 3: MLflow-tracked training + registry
│   │   └── evaluate.py          # Stage 4: held-out metrics + curves
│   ├── benchmarks/
│   │   └── latency.py           # HTTP latency benchmark harness
│   └── utils/                   # shared config/logging helpers
├── tests/                       # pytest suite (unit + benchmark smoke tests)
├── data/{raw,interim,processed}/  # DVC-tracked data (gitignored contents)
├── models/                      # DVC-tracked model artifacts
├── reports/                     # metrics, curves, latency results
├── dvc.yaml / params.yaml       # pipeline DAG + single source of config truth
├── Makefile                     # developer automation
└── pyproject.toml               # installable `payshap-ml` package
```

## Quickstart

```bash
make setup                 # create venv, install payshap-ml + dev deps
source .venv/bin/activate
make dvc-init               # initialize DVC + local storage remote (dev default)
# place a raw dataset at data/raw/transactions.parquet, matching the schema
# in src/payshap_ml/pipelines/data_ingestion.py::REQUIRED_COLUMNS
make reproduce               # dvc repro: prepare -> featurize -> train -> evaluate
make ml-ui                   # http://localhost:5000 — inspect runs & registered models
make serve                   # http://localhost:8000 — real-time scoring API
make test                    # pytest suite
make benchmark               # /predict latency benchmark (needs `make serve` running)
```

## Windows compatibility

The `Makefile` targets above assume a POSIX shell (`bin/` venv paths,
`find`, `rm -rf`). Two options on Windows:

**Recommended: WSL2.** Run this repo inside a WSL2 Ubuntu terminal and use
the Makefile exactly as documented above — DVC, MLflow, and the shell
utilities the Makefile relies on (`find`, `rm -rf`) are far better supported
and tested there than on native Windows.

**Native PowerShell alternative.** If you'd rather not use WSL2, the
Makefile also ships `-win` target variants using Windows-style paths
(`venv\Scripts\...`) that you can run with a Windows `make` install (e.g.
via `choco install make` / `scoop install make`), or just copy-paste
directly into PowerShell:

```powershell
# setup-win
python -m venv venv
.\venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -e ".[dev]"

# serve-win
uvicorn app.main:app --host 0.0.0.0 --port 8000
```

Equivalent `make ...-win` targets exist for `dvc-init`, `reproduce`,
`ml-ui`, `test`, and `benchmark` — see the "Windows (native PowerShell)
alternatives" section at the bottom of the `Makefile`. Note that DVC's S3
remote support and some MLflow file-locking behavior are less battle-tested
on native Windows, so if you hit issues there, WSL2 is the more reliable
path.

## Pipeline stages (`dvc.yaml`)

| Stage | Script | Output |
|---|---|---|
| `prepare` | `pipelines/data_ingestion.py` | `data/interim/cleaned.parquet` |
| `featurize` | `pipelines/feature_engineering.py` | `data/processed/{train,test}.parquet` |
| `train` | `pipelines/train.py` | `models/model.pkl`, MLflow run + registry entry |
| `evaluate` | `pipelines/evaluate.py` | `reports/eval_metrics.json`, PR/ROC curves |
| `benchmark` | `benchmarks/latency.py` | `reports/latency/latency_results.{json,csv}` |

Change any parameter in `params.yaml` and `dvc repro` will only re-run the
stages that depend on it.

## Feature engineering

PayShap/ISO 20022-specific features computed in `feature_engineering.py`,
all computed **causally** (no future information) to avoid label leakage:

- **ShapID velocity** — rolling count of a debtor's transfers in 5/15/60/1440
  minute windows.
- **Hourly transaction frequency** — cumulative count within the debtor's
  current hour-of-day bucket.
- **Time-delta** — seconds since the debtor's previous outgoing transfer.
- **Amount ratio to historical mean** — current amount vs. the debtor's
  trailing mean transfer amount.
- **ISO 20022 message attributes** — clearing system property, proxy type,
  agent BICs, purpose code, charge bearer (categorically encoded).

## Class imbalance

Fraud labels are assumed to be extremely rare (~0.1% positive). `train.py`
supports two strategies, selected via `model.imbalance_strategy` in
`params.yaml`:

- `scale_pos_weight` (default) — cost-sensitive learning built into
  LightGBM/XGBoost.
- `smote` — oversampling applied only to the training fold (never to
  validation/test) via `imbalanced-learn`.

## MLflow tracking & registry

Every training run logs: all `model.*` params, PR-AUC/ROC-AUC/precision/
recall/F1, a single-row latency sample, feature importances, and the fitted
model with an explicit `ModelSignature` + input example for inference-time
schema validation. Runs clearing `training.registry_threshold_pr_auc` are
registered to `models:/PayShap_Fraud_Detector/Staging`.

## Real-time serving (`app/main.py`)

- Loads the model from the MLflow Registry (`MODEL_STAGE`, default
  `Staging`) at startup via a `lifespan` context manager, falling back to
  the local `models/model.pkl` artifact if no MLflow server is reachable.
- `POST /predict` returns a fraud score and sets an `X-Inference-Time-MS`
  response header measuring in-process scoring time.
- **Known scaffold limitation**: `build_feature_row()` in `app/main.py`
  currently uses placeholder defaults for velocity/time-delta/amount-ratio
  features, since those require a low-latency online feature store keyed
  on `debtor_shap_id` (e.g. Redis) that is out of scope for this scaffold.
  Wire in a real feature-store lookup there before relying on this for
  actual fraud decisions — see the `TODO(feature-store)` comment.

## Latency benchmark harness

`src/payshap_ml/benchmarks/latency.py` load-tests the live `/predict`
endpoint with realistic synthetic PayShap payloads (varying amounts, proxy
IDs, timestamps — not empty/static JSON), and reports p50/p95/p99/avg
latency, throughput, error rate, and process RSS memory before/after.

**No benchmark numbers are hardcoded anywhere in this repository.** Every
figure in `reports/latency/latency_results.{json,csv}` comes from actually
executing HTTP requests against a running `/predict` endpoint at the time
`make benchmark` (or `dvc repro`'s `benchmark` stage) is run.

⚠️ **Latency results are environment-dependent.** Numbers from a laptop, a
CI runner, or a shared dev box are not representative of production and
must not be used for a go/no-go call on the <50ms budget. Always rerun this
harness on the actual target deployment hardware/network before trusting
its pass/fail verdict for a release decision.

Configuration lives under `benchmark:` in `params.yaml`:

```yaml
benchmark:
  target_rps: 100
  concurrency: 20
  warmup_runs: 200
  measured_runs: 2000
  timeout_ms: 500
  budgets:
    p95_latency_ms: 50
    max_error_rate: 0.01
```

The harness exits non-zero if the p95 budget or error-rate budget is
breached, so it can gate a CI job:

```yaml
# example CI step
- run: make serve &
- run: sleep 3 && make benchmark
```

### Running via `dvc repro`

`dvc.yaml`'s `deps:` list only drives cache invalidation — it does not
launch a server. Running the `benchmark` stage's HTTP client against a
port with nothing listening produces `error_rate=1.0000`, indistinguishable
from a real production outage. To make `dvc repro` self-contained, the
`benchmark` stage instead calls `scripts/run_benchmark_stage.py`, which:

1. launches `uvicorn app.main:app` as a subprocess,
2. polls `/health` until it's ready (or times out after 30s),
3. runs the benchmark harness,
4. tears the server down (success or failure) and propagates the exit code.

If you're iterating manually and want to watch server logs or keep it warm
between runs, use the two-terminal flow instead:

```bash
make serve            # terminal 1 — leave running
make benchmark          # terminal 2 — repeat as needed
```

`make benchmark-standalone` runs the same self-contained
start/wait/benchmark/teardown flow `dvc repro` uses, if you want it outside
of DVC.

## Testing

```bash
make test
```

Covers: data-cleaning edge cases (dedup, nulls, non-positive amounts),
feature engineering correctness (causal velocity/time-delta/ratio logic,
chronological split), and the benchmark harness (synthetic payload shape,
percentile math, report serialization, and an end-to-end smoke test
against a real in-process FastAPI server).
